//! The Sort module: ordering a view in place, for any element type, given a comparison.
//!
//! Written in Jairs like the rest of the standard library (PLAN.md decision #5). The **third** module, after
//! `Basic` and `String`, and the first that is *polymorphic* — so it is also the first library code to depend
//! on W5 rather than merely coexisting with it.
//!
//! # What made this expressible, all three probed before writing a line
//!
//! A `[]T` **view parameter is mutable** through the callee, so an in-place sort can exist at all. A `$T`
//! parameter **infers through a view** (`xs: []$T`, ADR-0084), so one routine serves every element type. And a
//! **procedure pointer** can be passed and called (ADR-0059), so the ordering can be a parameter. None of the
//! three was obvious from the plan, and writing the module first and discovering one missing would have meant
//! designing around a gap instead of knowing there was none.
//!
//! # Why the caller supplies the comparison
//!
//! `sort(xs, less)` takes a procedure rather than requiring `<` on the element type, and the reason is a
//! language fact rather than a taste: resolving an **operator** inside a `$T` template against the
//! instantiated type is a lookup polymorphic instantiation does not do. `operator <` exists (ADR-0048) and
//! `#modify` can *reject* an instantiation (ADR-0095), but nothing can *select* an implementation per
//! instantiated type. That would be operator-bounded polymorphism — a real feature, belonging to whichever
//! wave decides how a template states its requirements, not to a library that wants it.
//!
//! A comparison parameter is also the only form that works for a scalar **and** a struct with nothing the
//! language lacks, and it composes with `String.compare`, which the previous sub-wave shaped for exactly this.
//! `sort_ints` exists so the common case is still one word.
//!
//! # Why insertion sort, which is not a performance argument
//!
//! It is `O(n²)` and that is stated rather than hidden. The reasons to choose it *here* are that it is
//! **stable** — equal elements keep their relative order, which a caller can rely on and which quicksort does
//! not give — that it needs **no extra storage**, where a merge sort would need an allocation and allocation is
//! precisely what ADR-0103 §3 declined to decide, and that it is **short enough to read**, which matters for
//! the first sorting routine in a language whose test suite compares two independent engines against each
//! other.
//!
//! # And why `heap_sort` sits beside it rather than replacing it
//!
//! ADR-0146 §3 kept that promise: `heap_sort` is `O(n log n)` always, needs no storage, and is short — the
//! same three criteria, with the asymptotics moved from third place to first. It is **unstable**, and that is
//! why it is a second name rather than a faster `sort`.
//!
//! **Stability is observable behaviour, not an implementation quality.** With equal keys a stable and an
//! unstable sort produce different permutations, and a program that sorts by one field after sorting by
//! another depends on which it got. Swapping the algorithm silently would change what an existing program
//! computes, so the caller states which property they need — the same split Rust's `sort` and `sort_unstable`
//! make.
//!
//! Not a **hybrid** either (insertion below a threshold, heapsort above), which is what a production sort
//! does: it would make stability depend on the *input size*, so a program would work on small inputs and
//! silently reorder equal keys on large ones. Not a **merge sort**, which needs scratch storage and would
//! reopen the allocation decision ADR-0103 §3 declined to take. Not **quicksort**, whose worst case is
//! `O(n²)` on adversarial input and which needs a pivot argument.
//!
//! # Why every sorting routine counts its comparisons
//!
//! `comparisons: *s64` is not instrumentation bolted on: it is how the *choice between the two algorithms is
//! proved* (ADR-0146 §4). A comparison count is deterministic, machine-independent and identical in all three
//! engines, so `valid/117` asserting that heapsort makes strictly fewer comparisons than insertion sort on a
//! reversed input is a **test** that runs in the differential harness — where a wall-clock timing would be a
//! number needing a footnote about the machine it was taken on. It also measures the thing the decision rests
//! on, which is the asymptotics rather than the constant factor.
//!
//! Mandatory rather than optional, so there is no null check in the comparison loop and no second copy of
//! either algorithm. The `_ints` wrappers discard it, so the common case is unchanged.
//!
//! # Why there are `_ints` wrappers
//!
//! **Cross-file instantiation is deferred** (ADR-0082 §5). An importing file cannot instantiate `sort` or
//! `is_sorted` — the call is refused with E0268, which it did *not* used to be: a `$T` parameter's type is
//! `PoolId::ERROR` and `ERROR` matches anything, so the call type-checked and the missing instantiation leaked
//! out of an engine as "no routine for file 2 proc 0". Writing this module is what found that, and ADR-0104 §2
//! turned it into a diagnostic that names the workaround.
//!
//! The workaround is a wrapper *in the declaring module*, which is where the instantiation can happen. So
//! `sort_ints` and `ints_sorted` are not conveniences — they are the only way an importer can use this module
//! today, and they will become conveniences when cross-file instantiation arrives.

/// Sorts `xs` in place, ordering by `less`.
///
/// `less(a, b)` must answer "does `a` come strictly before `b`". A comparison that answers `true` for equal
/// elements would break stability — and worse, would make the result depend on the traversal order rather than
/// on the data, which is the kind of bug that reproduces on one engine and not the other.
///
/// **Stable**: equal elements keep their relative order, because the inner loop stops as soon as it finds an
/// element that is *not* strictly greater, so an equal one is never stepped over.
///
/// The comparison is called `O(n²)` times in the worst case. That is a property of insertion sort, chosen for
/// stability and for needing no storage rather than for speed — see the module docs.
sort :: (xs: []$T, less: (T, T) -> bool, comparisons: *s64) {
    // Insertion sort. `i` walks the boundary between the sorted prefix and the rest; each iteration takes one
    // element and moves it left past everything that should follow it.
    i := 1;
    while i < xs.count {
        current := xs[i];
        j := i;
        // Shift elements right while they belong after `current`. `j > 0` is checked first so the read at
        // `j - 1` is always in range — the bound is the loop's, not the array's, which is why no
        // `bounds_check` diagnostic is involved.
        keep_going := true;
        while keep_going {
            if j == 0 {
                keep_going = false;
            } else {
                comparisons.* = comparisons.* + 1;
                if less(current, xs[j - 1]) {
                    xs[j] = xs[j - 1];
                    j = j - 1;
                } else {
                    // Not strictly less, so `current` belongs here. Stopping on the *first* such element is
                    // what makes the sort stable: an equal element is never passed.
                    keep_going = false;
                }
            }
        }
        xs[j] = current;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

/// Sorts a view of `s64` ascending.
///
/// A named convenience so the common case does not have to spell a comparison. It exists because `sort` cannot
/// use `<` itself — see the module docs — and a caller writing `sort(xs, less_int)` for every integer sort
/// would be paying for a language limitation in every call.
sort_ints :: (xs: []s64) {
    n := 0;
    sort(xs, less_int, *n);
}

/// Sorts a view of `s64` ascending and reports how many comparisons it took.
///
/// Exists because **cross-file instantiation is deferred** (ADR-0104 §5): an importer cannot instantiate
/// `sort`, so a caller who wants the count can only get it from a wrapper this module instantiated. It is the
/// counted form of `sort_ints` and nothing else.
sort_ints_counting :: (xs: []s64, comparisons: *s64) {
    sort(xs, less_int, comparisons);
}

/// Sorts `xs` in place by `less`, in `O(n log n)` comparisons, **unstably**.
///
/// Heapsort: build a max-heap over the whole view, then repeatedly swap the root to the end and sift the new
/// root down through the shrinking heap. In place, no allocation, and `O(n log n)` in the *worst* case rather
/// than on average — see the module docs for why those are the criteria and why this does not replace `sort`.
///
/// **Not stable.** Equal elements may be reordered, because sifting moves an element past others of equal key.
/// A caller who needs stability wants `sort`.
///
/// `comparisons` is incremented once per call to `less`, which is what `valid/117` compares against
/// insertion sort's count (ADR-0146 §4).
heap_sort :: (xs: []$T, less: (T, T) -> bool, comparisons: *s64) {
    // **One loop, and one sift site, deliberately.** The textbook form is two phases — heapify, then
    // repeatedly extract — each calling a `sift_down` helper. That helper cannot exist here: a `$T`
    // template calling another `$T` template does not infer, even with the variable already bound
    // (`sift_down(xs, ...)` where `xs: []T` is E0268), which is a language limitation this module found
    // and PLAN §7 records. Writing the sift twice was the alternative and it is the duplication this
    // project keeps recording as a source of silent divergence, so the two phases are merged instead.
    //
    // `start` counts the heapify phase down from the last parent; once it reaches 0 the heap is built and
    // `end` counts the extraction phase down instead. Both phases then sift one root within `[0, end)`,
    // which is why there is one sift and not two.
    start := xs.count / 2;
    end := xs.count;
    while end > 1 {
        root := 0;
        if start > 0 {
            // Heapify: sift the next parent up the array, over the whole heap.
            start = start - 1;
            root = start;
        } else {
            // Extract: the root is the maximum, so swap it to the end and shrink the heap. The element
            // at `end` is then final and must not be touched again — which is what makes `end` the sift's
            // bound rather than `xs.count`.
            end = end - 1;
            top := xs[0];
            xs[0] = xs[end];
            xs[end] = top;
        }

        // Sift `root` down until both its children are no greater than it.
        parent := root;
        sifting := true;
        while sifting {
            child := parent * 2 + 1;
            if child >= end {
                // No children inside the heap, so the property holds here.
                sifting = false;
            } else {
                // The larger of the two children, so the parent ends up above both.
                largest := child;
                right := child + 1;
                if right < end {
                    comparisons.* = comparisons.* + 1;
                    if less(xs[largest], xs[right]) {
                        largest = right;
                    }
                }
                comparisons.* = comparisons.* + 1;
                if less(xs[parent], xs[largest]) {
                    held := xs[parent];
                    xs[parent] = xs[largest];
                    xs[largest] = held;
                    parent = largest;
                } else {
                    sifting = false;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

/// Sorts a view of `s64` ascending with `heap_sort`.
///
/// The wrapper exists for `sort_ints`' reason, unchanged (ADR-0104 §5).
heap_sort_ints :: (xs: []s64) {
    n := 0;
    heap_sort(xs, less_int, *n);
}

/// Sorts a view of `s64` ascending with `heap_sort`, reporting the comparison count.
heap_sort_ints_counting :: (xs: []s64, comparisons: *s64) {
    heap_sort(xs, less_int, comparisons);
}

/// Whether a view of `s64` is ordered ascending.
///
/// The non-polymorphic counterpart of `is_sorted`, and it exists for the same reason `sort_ints` does:
/// **cross-file instantiation is deferred** (ADR-0082 §5, refused with E0268 since ADR-0104 §2), so an
/// importer cannot instantiate `is_sorted` — only call a wrapper this module instantiated for it. That is a
/// real limitation of the language today rather than a stylistic choice, and naming it here is better than
/// letting a caller discover it as a diagnostic.
ints_sorted :: (xs: []s64) -> bool {
    return is_sorted(xs, less_int);
}

/// Whether `xs` is ordered by `less`.
///
/// Useful to a caller checking a precondition, and to a *test* checking a postcondition — which is the honest
/// reason it is here: a sort's correctness is "the result is ordered and holds the same elements", and this is
/// the first half stated once rather than open-coded at every call site that wants it.
///
/// An empty or single-element view is ordered, which follows from there being no adjacent pair to violate.
is_sorted :: (xs: []$T, less: (T, T) -> bool) -> bool {
    i := 1;
    while i < xs.count {
        // Ordered means "no element comes strictly before its predecessor". Written this way round rather than
        // as `less(xs[i - 1], xs[i])` because the latter is *false* for equal neighbours, which would call a
        // correctly sorted view with duplicates unsorted.
        if less(xs[i], xs[i - 1]) {
            return false;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Ascending order for `s64`.
///
/// Exported rather than module-private, because a caller composing it — sorting descending with a wrapper, or
/// passing it to `is_sorted` to check a postcondition — needs to name it. Hiding it would make `sort_ints` the
/// only way to use the ordering, which is a narrower module for no benefit.
less_int :: (a: s64, b: s64) -> bool {
    return a < b;
}
